/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 730 Tm
([Abstract]) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 702 Tm
(LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 669 Tm
(Authors: Edward J. Hu, Yelong Shen, Phillip Wallis, Zeyuan Allen-Zhu, Yuanzhi Li,) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 654 Tm
(Shean Wang, Lu Wang, Weizhu Chen \(2021\)) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 621 Tm
(Abstract: An important paradigm of natural language processing consists of) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 606 Tm
(large-scale pre-training on general domain data and adaptation to particular tasks) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 591 Tm
(or domains. As we pre-train larger models, full fine-tuning, which retrains all) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 576 Tm
(model parameters, becomes less feasible. Using GPT-3 175B as an example, deploying) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 561 Tm
(independent instances of fine-tuned models, each with 175B parameters, is) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 546 Tm
(prohibitively expensive.) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 513 Tm
(We propose Low-Rank Adaptation, or LoRA, which freezes the pre-trained model weights) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 498 Tm
(and injects trainable rank decomposition matrices into each layer of the Transformer) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 483 Tm
(architecture, greatly reducing the number of trainable parameters for downstream) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 468 Tm
(tasks. Compared to GPT-3 175B fine-tuned with Adam, LoRA can reduce the number of) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 453 Tm
(trainable parameters by 10,000 times and the GPU memory requirement by 3-fold.) Tj
ET
endstream
endobj
8 0 obj
<< /Length 1402 >>
stream
BT
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 730 Tm
([Low-Rank Parameterized Update Matrices]) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 702 Tm
(4.1 Low-Rank Parameterized Update Matrices: A neural network contains many dense) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 687 Tm
(layers which perform matrix multiplication. The weight matrices in these layers) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 672 Tm
(typically have full-rank. When adapting to a specific task, pre-trained language) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 657 Tm
(models have a low intrinsic dimension and can still learn efficiently despite a) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 642 Tm
(random projection to a smaller subspace.) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 609 Tm
(For a pre-trained weight matrix W_0 in R^\(d x k\), we constrain its update by) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 594 Tm
(representing the latter with a low-rank decomposition W_0 + Delta W = W_0 + B A,) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 579 Tm
(where B in R^\(d x r\), A in R^\(r x k\), and the rank r << min\(d, k\). During training,) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 564 Tm
(W_0 is frozen and does not receive gradient updates, while A and B contain trainable) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 549 Tm
(parameters.) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 516 Tm
(We use a random Gaussian initialization for A and zero for B, so Delta W = B A is) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 501 Tm
(zero at the beginning of training. Unlike Adapters, LoRA introduces zero additional) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 486 Tm
(inference latency because the trainable matrices B A can be explicitly merged with) Tj
1 0 0 1 54 471 Tm
(the frozen weights W_0 during deployment.) Tj
ET
endstream
endobj
